&lt;h2&gt;Running it in practice&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Mutation testing is expensive. A suite that takes a minute to run might need to run hundreds of times, once per mutant. A few habits keep it practical:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Scope it.&lt;/strong&gt; Start with a single module or a critical directory rather than the whole repository.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Run incrementally.&lt;/strong&gt; Only mutate files changed in the current branch and cache results between runs.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Use coverage data.&lt;/strong&gt; Skip mutants on lines no test executes; they will trivially survive and add noise.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Run in CI on a schedule.&lt;/strong&gt; A nightly or weekly full run is often a better fit than blocking every pull request.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Parallelise.&lt;/strong&gt; Mutants are independent, so they split cleanly across workers.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;Reading the results&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A surviving mutant is a question, not a verdict. Before writing a new test, ask:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Is this a genuine gap in my assertions?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Is the mutant &lt;em&gt;equivalent&lt;/em&gt;, meaning it changes the code without changing observable behaviour (for example, &lt;code&gt;i &amp;lt; 10&lt;/code&gt; versus &lt;code&gt;i != 10&lt;/code&gt; in a loop that always exits earlier)?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Is the code itself dead, and should it simply be deleted?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Equivalent mutants are the main source of false positives. You can&#39;t eliminate them entirely, which is why chasing a 100% mutation score is rarely worth it. A score in the 70–85% range on important code is a healthy target for many teams.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;Where it fits&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Mutation testing doesn&#39;t replace code coverage, property-based testing, or code review. It complements them by answering a different question: &lt;em&gt;if this code were wrong, would my tests tell me?&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Used selectively on the logic that matters most, such as pricing, permissions, and data transformations, it tends to surface a handful of high-value missing assertions rather than a mountain of busywork. That is usually enough to justify the runtime.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;Further reading&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The documentation for your language&#39;s mutation testing tool, such as Stryker for JavaScript and TypeScript, PIT for Java, or mutmut for Python.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Research on the &lt;em&gt;coupling effect&lt;/em&gt;, which argues that tests that catch simple mutants also tend to catch more complex faults.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Your own test suite: pick one critical function, mutate it by hand, and see whether anything fails.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
